[Opening "Scandinavian Defense: Main Line, Mieses Variation"]

1. e4 d5 { I'm not sure why this opening exists but it's common enough that we
should elaborate on the pain that we will inflict upon black's soul. } 2. exd5 Qxd5 (2... Nf6 { If I had to play the Scandi I'd at least try this. } 3. d4 Nxd5 (3... Bg4 4. Nf3 Nxd5 { transposes }) 4. Nf3 { what's nice about this move is that
we have a simple plan against Bg4 - really the only move we should be concerned
with } 4... Bg4 5. Be2 { We intend Ne5 at our convenience. SPARRING POSITI $146 } 5... e6 6. O-O Be7 7. h3 Bh5 (7... Bxf3 8. Bxf3 $16 { This loss of the light squares is
common in the Scandi. Our basic plan here is simple: after black loses a tempo
dealing with the threat of c4 we will put our pawns on dark squares to
compliment our unopposed bishop (c3) and start bringing our knight in. Black's
long term problem is that it's always bad news if they open the position for our
monster Bf3. }) 8. Ne5 { note that we have a similar plan against the Alehkine } 8... Bxe2 9. Qxe2 { With a very unpleasant position for black, they'd like to
trade on e5 but then a big fat pawn arrives on e5 and our rook is going to d1
anyway. Note that when they take on e5 we will attack the kingside like in a
French structure. }) 3. Nc3 Qa5 (3... Qd8 { such a sad move } 4. d4 Nf6 5. Nf3 Bg4 { The only move we should be concerned about as black needs to trade some pieces
to relieve his cramp. } (5... c6 6. Bc4 { With a great attack because Bg4 isn't
playable. } 6... Bg4 $2 { [%c_effect g4;square;g4;type;Mistake;persistent;true] } 7. Bxf7+ Kxf7 8. Ne5+ $18 { Ye olde trick. }) 6. h3 Bxf3 (6... Bh5 7. g4 Bg6 8. Ne5 { The light square bishop is now a victim. } 8... e6 9. Bg2 c6 10. h4 $18 { h5 is a
crushing threat }) 7. Qxf3 c6 8. Be3 $16 { With a nice lead in development. Our long
term goal will be to open the position for our unopposed light square bishop. }) (3... Qd6 { Around 2005 the freaks of the world waved this flag } 4. d4 Nf6 5. Nf3 { SPARRING POSITI $146 } 5... Bg4 (5... a6 6. g3 { a6 works against Bc4 with its b5
intention, so we go the other way and enable a later Bf4. } 6... Bg4 7. Bg2 Nc6 8. O-O O-O-O 9. h3 $1 { wtih a big attack } { [%c_effect h3;square;h3;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] } 9... Bxf3 10. Qxf3 Qxd4 11. Rd1 Qc4 12. Bg5 { Black is going
to get mated. Note the power our unopposed bishop on g2, it's a common asset vs
the Scandi. }) 6. h3 { We also ask this question in the main line. What we need to
understand is that the problem with black retreating is they will then get
rolled up. } 6... Bxf3 (6... Bh5 7. g4 Bg6 8. Bg2 Nbd7 9. O-O { Black can try
several moves here, c6, e6, 0-0-0 but our plan is the same: Nh4, f4-f5.
clickable variation } 9... c6 10. Nh4 e6 11. f4 O-O-O 12. f5 exf5 13. Nxf5 Bxf5 14. Rxf5 Nb6 15. g5 $16 { With a lovely initiative. Note again the familiar
long-term plan: open the position for our unopposed light square bishop. }) 7. Qxf3 c6 8. Bf4 $1 { hello } { [%c_effect f4;square;f4;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] } 8... Qxd4 9. Nb5 $1 $18 { [%c_effect b5;square;b5;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] } 9... Qxb2 (9... cxb5 { UPDATE } 10. Bxb5+ Nbd7 11. Qxb7 { with a Morphy pin on the
d7 knight $1 } 11... Rd8 12. Bc7 $18 { Like in Morphy-Allies 1854 the R will land on
d1 and black doesn't have enough time to unravel. }) 10. Nc7+ Kd8 11. Rd1+ Nbd7 12. Nxa8 $18 { Black has lost their coconuts. }) 4. d4 Nf6 5. Nf3 c6 { This is the
move Scandi players love. } (5... Bg4 { The most terrible thing about the Scandi
is that this natural move (trade some pieces, consolidate) is not so hot } 6. h3 Bh5 (6... Bxf3 7. Qxf3 c6 8. Bd2 Nbd7 9. O-O-O e6 10. Kb1 { We will be looking
to play g4/g5 and then h4-h5 with a strong attack. Again: look to open lines for
our unopposed light square bishop. }) 7. g4 Bg6 8. Ne5 Nbd7 (8... c6 { Black's
queen needs a way out, but it's a big tempo to lose here. } 9. h4 Nbd7 10. Nc4 Qc7 11. h5 Be4 12. Nxe4 Nxe4 13. Qf3 Nd6 14. Bf4 $18 { We castle long next and
enjoy all the advantages in the position, especially having a safer king. }) 9. Nc4 { Black's queen is going to be uncomfortable. } 9... Qa6 { Obviously not a
comfortable square. Most Scandi players have trained themselves to lose a tempo
with c6 to avoid this kind of hot mess. } 10. Bf4 { threatening Nd6 } 10... Qe6+ 11. Ne5 $18 { Black is lost because his f8 bishop looks like it got buried in an
avalanche. }) 6. Bd2 { Simple chess, Qa5 will owe us more time. \"Don't develop
your queen early.\" SPARRING POSITI $146 } 6... Bg4 (6... Bf5 7. Bc4 { The threat of
Nd5 jumpstarts our attack. } 7... Nbd7 (7... e6 8. Qe2 Bxc2 (8... Nbd7 { transposes to 7...Nbd7 }) 9. d5 $18 { black will not survive this one }) 8. Qe2 e6 (8... Bxc2 9. Nb5 Qd8 10. Nd6#) 9. d5 $1 { All of our pieces will be working
and black isn't going to castle easily. } { [%c_effect d5;square;d5;type;GreatFind;persistent;true] }) 7. h3 Bh5 8. g4 Bg6 9. Ne5 Nbd7 10. f4 e6 11. h4 { A common problem for black in the Scandi: Bg6 and the light squares
are a victim. } *